# 11. CLASSIFICATION TARGETS

Unknown fields may resolve to:

```text
definition
concept
related
code
summary
interview
warning
memory
note
generic
```

Title/subtitle discovery should be handled separately where appropriate.

If `generic` does not currently exist in `SemanticBlockType`, add it safely.

---

# 12. GENERIC IS IMPORTANT

Do NOT force uncertain content into an incorrect semantic category.

Example:

```json
{
  "browserSupport": "Supported by all modern browsers."
}
```

Expected:

```text
type:
generic

title:
Browser Support

content:
Supported by all modern browsers.
```

Rule:

> Unknown content should be preserved, not dropped and not falsely classified.

Generic is a successful result.

---

# 13. CLASSIFICATION RESULT

Create approximately:

```ts
interface FieldClassification {
  semanticType: SemanticBlockType

  confidence: number

  reasons: string[]
}
```

Confidence:

```text
0.0 → 1.0
```

Example:

```text
Field:
importantPoints

Type:
concept

Confidence:
0.92

Reasons:
- key tokens match concept aliases
- value is a string array
- items are short explanatory statements
```

Keep reasons deterministic.

---

# 14. CLASSIFICATION SCORING

Do not use one giant `if/else`.

Calculate scores for candidate semantic types.

Conceptually:

```text
Definition Score
Concept Score
Code Score
Summary Score
Warning Score
Memory Score
Interview Score
Related Score
Note Score
```

Signals can add/subtract points.

Example:

```text
key alias match
+0.55

compatible value shape
+0.20

compatible content length
+0.10

supporting parent-path signal
+0.10

contradictory value shape
-0.20
```

Exact numbers can be adjusted during implementation.

Keep scoring centralized and understandable.

---

# 15. CONFIDENCE THRESHOLDS

Centralize thresholds.

Recommended starting behavior:

```text
confidence >= 0.80
→ strong semantic match

confidence >= 0.60
→ acceptable semantic match

confidence < 0.60
→ generic
```

Tune if fixtures show better deterministic values.

Do NOT aggressively classify weak matches.

---

# 16. TITLE DISCOVERY

Explicit:

```text
title
```

always wins.

Otherwise support conservative title aliases:

```text
topic
subject
heading
name
```

Example:

```json
{
  "topic": "JavaScript Closures"
}
```

should likely produce:

```text
document.title = "JavaScript Closures"
```

Requirements:

* preferably top-level
* scalar string
* reasonably short
* strong title alias

Do not simply use the first string in the JSON.

---

# 17. SUBTITLE DISCOVERY

Explicit subtitle wins.

Possible aliases:

```text
subtitle
tagline
subheading
context
```

Infer only with reasonable confidence.

Subtitle remains optional.

